WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.352
Scenario: You receive a password-reset SMS for a service

2
00:00:03.352 --> 00:00:06.703
while you are not attempting to reset your password.

3
00:00:06.703 --> 00:00:10.055
A second message asks you to approve the reset.

4
00:00:10.055 --> 00:00:13.407
Safest response: Option B: Ignore the link, navigate independently

5
00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:16.759
to the service and review account security or contact

6
00:00:16.759 --> 00:00:20.110
support if needed. Unsolicited reset messages should be verified

7
00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.462
through the official service rather than followed. Option A

8
00:00:23.462 --> 00:00:27.186
is unsafe because The code may authorize an attacker-controlled reset.

9
00:00:27.186 --> 00:00:30.538
Option C is unsafe because Approving a reset you

10
00:00:30.538 --> 00:00:34.634
did not request may give another party control of the account.

11
00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.986
Option D is unsafe because The link could lead

12
00:00:37.986 --> 00:00:41.338
to a credential-harvesting site. In practice: Do not open

13
00:00:41.338 --> 00:00:44.690
the SMS link or share a code. Access the

14
00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:48.041
service independently and report repeated or suspicious recovery activity.

15
00:00:48.041 --> 00:00:51.393
Key takeaway: Only initiate recovery yourself through the official

16
00:00:51.393 --> 00:00:54.000
service; do not approve an unsolicited reset.
